{"translation": "For where our task is to fill a sinus with flesh, we melt it in a large quantity of oil and pour it in through a straight-bored horn joined, in the manner of a clyster, [500] to a pig's bladder; we also use for this the so-called pus-drawers (pyulci). Of these too, those with a wider bore are serviceable for such a purpose. It is evident that we make a foul sinus clean by using the drug less diluted, that is, mixing it with less oil, and a clean one by using it more moistened through the admixture of more plentiful oil. If, then, there should be some suspicion of a slight inflammation still mixed in with the sinus, let the drug by all means be melted in rose oil. But if the sinus is free of inflammation, let it be melted in oil; and if it tolerates the admixture of old oil without being bitten, let the drug be dissolved in that. Suitable for such use is not only this drug but also that of Machairion and the isis, and all the others which my predecessors have written become flesh-producing for hollow ulcers when mixed with cerate.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 18, "surface": "κύστει", "english": "bladder", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 72, "surface": "ὑποψία", "english": "suspicion", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 89, "surface": "ἀφλέγμαντος", "english": "free of inflammation", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 109, "surface": "χρῆσίν", "english": "use", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 120, "surface": "Μαχαιρίωνος", "english": "of Machairion", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 132, "surface": "κηρωτῆς", "english": "cerate", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 134, "surface": "σαρκωτικὰ", "english": "flesh-producing", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 136, "surface": "κοίλων", "english": "hollow", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["πυουλκοῖς", "τοῦ παλαιοῦ", "ὑγρασμένῳ", "ἐπ’ αὐτοῦ"]}
