{"translation": "secondly, to have been trained in this [459] way on many particular cases, knowing that without having gone through such training none of the tasks is carried out correctly. Terrible, too, is what has already befallen certain people, two physicians whom I know. For the one, wasting away from grief at the loss of such writings, died, and the other withdrew from medical practice. Those who have been trained in the way I describe have, besides being able to compound useful drugs, also the capacity to be critical judges of what was written by the physicians before us, and to make suitable use both of everything written by them and of whatever one discovers oneself according to the method that is going to be taught. It is better, then, to use those already tested through experience, once one has learned the method of their use; but, as I said, necessity often overtakes us, so that we also use untried ones, improvising. In the book before this one, then, it has been stated how one might prepare plasters from litharge alone, mixing in sometimes oil alone, and sometimes vinegar or wine or some of these, and from what proportion of them a white plaster can be produced when properly boiled.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 36, "surface": "ἰατροῖν.", "english": "two physicians", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 54, "surface": "ἰατρικῶν", "english": "medical", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 71, "surface": "κριτικοῖς", "english": "critical judges", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 95, "surface": "μέθοδον.", "english": "method", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 96, "surface": "βέλτιον", "english": "better", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 137, "surface": "λιθαργύρου", "english": "litharge", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 139, "surface": "ἐμπλάστρους", "english": "plasters", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 160, "surface": "ἔμπλαστρος", "english": "plaster", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["ταύτων (read as τούτων, 'some of these'; possibly ταὐτῶν)", "τὸ