{"translation": "It is clear, then, that being of this kind it will not incarnate the ulcer nor fill up the hollow of the flesh, but will both melt it down and diminish it; and for this reason the drugs that cleanse much filth [554], about which I spoke earlier, are not always able to incarnate, but only when, having been melted either with a larger quantity of oil or with rose oil, as are both the 'oily' drug and the one made with papyrus, the vehemence of their detergent action is curbed, or when through the admixture of cerate they become non-biting. Many of the polychrest remedies are of this kind, such as that of Machairion and the Isis. Now the aim of each of these has been stated in the books of the Therapeutic Method; and as for the material of the remedies, the simple material was stated earlier in another treatise, and the compound is stated in the present one. And first of all in it one must bear in mind the nature of the bodies being treated. Those that are moist, and for this reason soft, such as those of women and small children, and of eunuchs and of those who live in a womanish manner, require weaker drugs; but those of men, and especially of all who through manly activities and the sun have acquired a hard habit of body, are not benefited at all by the weak ones.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 48, "surface": "ἐλαιῶδες", "english": "'oily'", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 57, "surface": "ῥύψεως", "english": "detergent action", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 60, "surface": "κηρωτῆς", "english": "cerate", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 68, "surface": "πολυχρήστων", "english": "polychrest", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 69, "surface": "δυνάμεών", "english": "remedies", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 75, "surface": "Μαχαιρίωνος", "english": "that of Machairion", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 143, "surface": "ἀσθενεστέρων", "english": "weaker", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 154, "surface": "ἐνεργειῶν", "english": "activities", "uncertain"