{"translation": "But rarely a little more or less than that. And so that the preparation should have adhesion, oiliness and viscosity, and should not be friable, nor easily dissolved, nor apt to fall away from the skin, I mixed resin into it: preferably genuine terebinth, if it was to hand, and if not, at least the most readily obtainable kind, which they call [624] roasted and Colophonian. Let its weight be equal to that of the wax. For it preserves, in whatever drugs it is put into, the consistency they already had, provided that it too is of moderate consistency. For it is evident that, as in all other things, so too in resin there is the more and the less. If, then, it ever appears oilier and moister, one must recognise that the cerate that will be made from wax melted in oil will be moister than it would otherwise be. In general, in the case of the nerve-wounded, whenever one misses the exact and middle consistency of plaster-like drugs, it is better to lean toward the softer, even if the preparation is going to be one that smears. And likewise also the one that is moderate in consistency. Pitch preserves the consistency of the cerate. As I said, the best for such a mixture is the oily kind, just as with resin too. If you put in a liquid resin, such as terebinth is in consistency, its amount should properly be a third part of the wax; and of terebinth itself, the fresh is much moister than the old.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 27, "surface": "ῥητίνης", "english": "resin", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 33, "surface": "τερμινθίνης", "english": "terebinth", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 46, "surface": "κολοφωνίαν.", "english": "Colophonian", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 85, "surface": "ἧττόν", "english": "the less", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 104, "surface": "κηρωτὴν", "english": "cerate", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 111, "surface": "νευροτρώτων,", "english": "the nerve-wounded", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 148, "surface": "κηρωτῆς", "e