{"translation": "But if [690] it should be hard, it will be necessary to add something of the liquids as well. It is for this reason, then, that some, it seems to me, have also in such drugs added a qualification to the wax, some writing 'new', others 'fresh'. They were not, by Zeus, requiring its age, as in the case of other drugs, but its moistness. For new wax is indeed better, but not so much that one should already shun wax two or three years old. It is time to go on to the plaster written next after this one, which runs as follows. ℞ Diphryges, 84 dr.; litharge, 240 dr.; wax, 318 dr.; oil, 6 cotylae. This drug would be cicatrizing, for ulcers not yet malignant but already hard-to-cicatrise. For it dries, but not strongly, as does the one written after it. Nor, indeed, is it fitting to prepare it in so large a quantity. For this is the same as it: ℞ Diphryges, 14 dr.; litharge, 40 dr.; wax, 53 dr.; myrtle oil, 10 ounces. For the amounts written by Andromachus are sixfold of these. This, then, is truly cicatrizing, similar to the one made with cadmia and to the one called the Sweet [plaster], about which we shall speak in the following book.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 15, "surface": "ἔνιοι", "english": "some", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 60, "surface": "τριῶν", "english": "three", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 68, "surface": "ἔμπλαστρον", "english": "plaster", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 92, "surface": "ἐπουλωτικὸν,", "english": "cicatrizing", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 99, "surface": "δυσεπουλώτων.", "english": "hard-to-cicatrise", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 139, "surface": "ἑξαπλάσια", "english": "sixfold", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 157, "surface": "καδμείας", "english": "cadmia", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 160, "surface": "γλυκείᾳ", "english": "Sweet", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["γο (abbreviation, taken as οὐγγίαι, 'ounces')", "κο. (abbreviation, taken as κοτύλαι)", "προσγεγραφέναι τῷ κηρῷ ... τοῦ νέου ..